ECE = """\
Modern automobiles are complex electro-mechanical systems. Electronics and Communication Engineering plays an important role in automotive R&D and production quality at Tata Motors. A single vehicle may contain dozens of electronic control units coordinating engine or motor torque, braking, lighting, climate control, instrument cluster, infotainment, and safety features.

Key domains include engine control units (ECU), body control modules, infotainment and telematics, ADAS sensors, battery management systems (BMS) in EVs, wiring harnesses, in-vehicle networks (CAN, LIN, Ethernet in newer architectures), and over-the-air software updates. Poor harness routing or weak earthing can cause intermittent faults; therefore production plants enforce standard work instructions, torque tools, and electrical tests.

During authorised plant visits or internships, diploma students may observe trim-and-chassis (TCF) processes, harness routing, earthing safety checks, ECU flashing, heating-element quality, connector inspection, PLC-based automation, robotic welding cells, and end-of-line electrical testing—especially at passenger vehicle plants such as Pune and manufacturing locations such as Sanand and Jamshedpur. Documenting these observations with diagrams and safety rules strengthens the college internship report."""

PLANTS = """\
Tata Motors operates major manufacturing and assembly facilities in India including Pune (passenger vehicles), Jamshedpur and Lucknow (commercial vehicles), Sanand (passenger and EV-related capacity), and Dharwad. Plants are organised into shops: press shop (stamping panels), body shop (welding and framing), paint shop (corrosion protection and aesthetics), engine or battery assembly (where applicable), trim and final assembly, and vehicle testing.

Material flow uses conveyors, AGVs, and kitting stations aligned with takt time. Quality gates stop defects from propagating downstream. Environmental controls in paint shops and strict high-voltage procedures in EV lines protect workers and product integrity. Capacity expansions—such as Sanand scale-up and the Tamil Nadu greenfield—support new models and export ambitions."""

